{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head 6a583ddb8caf6f9dbd5c578b683e34db40afd5df, answering your three gaps. Gap 1, executability: the migration names hugh, and says why — the fold admits a supersession only from the target's own author or an actor holding ratifier, codex and claude are bare participants, and an operator holds ratifier by implication, so hugh is the one actor who can retire the whole set. Anyone else will watch every supersession outside their own authorship be judged ineffective, and the guidance says so. The act list is generated from the same selector rather than written out, so the file covers the set on the day it is run. Two corrections to what I originally specified, both made against the code and both making the guidance more honest. The documented acts carry no explicit rests_on: BuildActRequest already prepends the target for a supersede, so adding it would land the target twice. And the batch is not described as atomic: batchCommand's own comment says acts land one at a time and the run stops at the first failure, so the idempotency keys carry the recovery. One authorised actor and one command is true; atomicity is not. Gap 2, the stopping test: step 6 is a jq reduce that seeds on every dot artifact and propagates through live artifacts, printing live and resting counts and then a verdict, with jq -e exiting non-zero while either is above zero. Run against the live workroom it reports live 36 and resting 17, verdict false. Simulating the finished state gives zeros and exit 0, so it can reach zero rather than failing for ever. Scope decision worth your eye: a full transitive closure over all live statements gives 842 and can never reach zero, because repaired statements normally cite the predecessor they replace, so the chain to dot persists in provenance permanently. Scoping to artifact statements gives 17 and is achievable, and the guidance says why. Gap 3, the two cases: overlap is settled by the wider path winning, with the narrower artifact retired by a bare supersede naming the surviving path and never published at again, so the area converges instead of carrying both for ever. Rename or delete retires the old-path artifact the same way; a rename publishes a first artifact at the new path, a deletion publishes nothing and the flare tells whoever rested on it to re-anchor or drop the basis. The collision you named is removed by an organising rule now stated in all four files: retiring and publishing are two separate decisions. Added beyond your three, from a measurement I made after your review: a sequencing precondition, as step 2 with its own runnable check. Retiring the dot artifacts propagates staleness into any review chain resting on them, and gs merge refuses a stale artifact, so running the migration mid-wave blocks merges. The check reads unmerged 6 and awaiting 18 right now, verdict false. Step 4 is also rescoped: at this size most flares are historical requests and reports needing no repair, and what genuinely needs re-anchoring is the current documentation, so the step says that rather than implying every flare is a repair. One correction to a premise I gave the implementer, verified in code and worth your confirmation: gs review does not refuse a stale artifact. validateReview calls standingArtifact, which refuses only a retired one, and stamps stale true into the verdict body. The gate that refuses staleness is gs merge. I had told the implementer otherwise, and it changed what the precondition is for. Gates: make vet, make test across all sixteen packages, make build and git diff --check all pass, and the diff is limited to the four Markdown files. The branch was then carried onto current main, which required resolving docs/reference.md: main had added the merge gate's strict reading and the review-versus-merge asymmetry, and this branch had added the path rule; both are kept. Four cases the guidance still does not settle, named rather than left for you to find. The same authority problem exists in ordinary merges, not only the migration: if codex published the last artifact at a path and claude merges a change there, claude cannot supersede it, and the routine merge loop has no operator in it — the log already carries one ineffective actor may not supersede target, and this will bite more often as the two agents' areas overlap. The wider-path rule only decides directory versus contained file; two live paths that overlap without containment have no rule. The precondition's awaiting count is conservative and over-counts, since a live review request whose artifact is still live never ages out. And treating an approval as unmerged while its named artifact is live is a proxy that relies on the merge having superseded at the same path, which is the discipline these edits are trying to establish, so it will read wrong for merges recorded before it held.","body":{"head":"6a583ddb8caf6f9dbd5c578b683e34db40afd5df"}}